course: dictionary
lesson: 14250
title: A word that has changed
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words were once normal but are now hurtful, so people stop using them. A dictionary marks these words as outdated or offensive, so you know not to use them to describe people. «Negro» is one of these words: it was once used in English for Black people, but today it is considered offensive.
explain: You may still meet it in old books, speeches and names from history. In the USA, «Negro Leagues» were baseball teams for Black players, and «Negro spirituals» are old religious songs. These names show how language was used in the past, so they are kept as they are.
explain: Context helps here too. When you read an old text, look at the date and the topic, then ask whether the word describes a person or names something from history. Today, respectful words for people are «Black» or «African American» in the USA, and «Black British» in the UK.
explain: «Negro» also means something different in Spanish and Portuguese, where it means black. That is why you find it in place names such as the Rio Negro, which means «Black River» and is a great river in South America.
fact: «Negro» was once used in English for a Black person, but it is now offensive and is not used to describe people.
fact: Dictionaries mark words like this as outdated or offensive, so you know to avoid them.
fact: «Negro Leagues» were baseball leagues for Black players in the USA, and the name is kept because it is a historical name.
fact: In Spanish and Portuguese, «negro» means «black», as in the Rio Negro, or «Black River».
fact: Today, say «Black» or «African American» in the USA, or «Black British» in the UK.
exercise: Is «negro» used to describe people today? | Yes, but only in history books about people | Yes, it is the polite word to use today | *No, it is outdated and offensive | Only in Spain
exercise: In Spanish and Portuguese, what does «negro» mean? | Quiet | White | Cold | *Black
exercise: Which respectful word is used today for a Black person in the UK? | *Black British | Old | Negro | Dark
test: «The Rio Negro is a great river in South America.» What does negro mean in this name? | Blue | *Black | Long | Fast
test: Why do we keep names like «Negro Leagues» in history lessons? | *They are historical names that show how language was used | Because they are sports rules | Because they are in Spanish | Because the word is polite today
exam: Why should we stop using «negro» to describe people today? | *It is now considered offensive, so we use respectful words such as «Black» | It is a Spanish word | It is too short | It only means dark brown
